---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/docs.astro";
---

<DocsLayout title="The five AI assistance modes — apprenti.dev Docs">
    <div class="space-y-8">
        <div>
            <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold">
                The five AI assistance modes
            </h1>
        </div>

        <div class="prose dark:prose-invert max-w-none space-y-8">
            <section>
                <p class="text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">
                    On any task's detail screen, AI assistance shows up as up
                    to five distinct modes — not one generic chat. Each one
                    has a narrow, specific job, and each one is switched on
                    or off independently by that task's AI policy. This page
                    covers what each mode actually does; see
                    <a href="/docs/apprentice-using-ai-assistance" class="text-link hover:underline">Using AI assistance</a>
                    for how the AI tab, providers, and privacy work in
                    general.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section>
                <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-3">Explain</h2>
                <p class="text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">
                    Prompt: <em>"Explain this task."</em> Explains the
                    concepts behind the task without writing the solution
                    for you. This is the closest thing to a starting point —
                    reach for it when the instructions make sense but the
                    underlying idea doesn't yet.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section>
                <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-3">Hint</h2>
                <p class="text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">
                    Prompt: <em>"Give me a hint for this task."</em> Gives a
                    progressive hint and deliberately stops short of solving
                    the assignment. Use it when you know roughly what to do
                    but are stuck on the next step, not when you want the
                    answer.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section>
                <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-3">Quiz</h2>
                <p class="text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">
                    Prompt: <em>"Quiz me on this task."</em> Asks one
                    Socratic question about the task. Answer it in the same
                    thread and the next reply evaluates what you said — what
                    was right, what to revisit — and asks a follow-up
                    question, the same way a real back-and-forth quiz would.
                    It never writes the assignment for you along the way.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section>
                <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-3">Coach</h2>
                <p class="text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">
                    Prompt: <em>"Help me reflect on this task."</em> Coaches
                    a short reflection rather than doing the reflecting for
                    you — useful right before you write your own reflection
                    for submission, to think through what you actually
                    learned instead of staring at a blank field.
                </p>
                <p class="text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">
                    Coach is <strong>off by default</strong>. If it's not
                    showing up on a task, that's very likely deliberate —
                    ask your mentor or the curriculum author if you think it
                    should be available.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section>
                <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-3">Solution</h2>
                <p class="text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">
                    Prompt: <em>"Write the full solution for this task."</em>
                    The one mode that can actually produce a complete,
                    finished solution. It's the exception, not the rule —
                    Explain, Hint, Quiz, and Coach are all built to
                    deliberately stop short of this.
                </p>
                <p class="text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">
                    Solution is <strong>off by default</strong>, controlled
                    by its own separate policy setting (not the same list
                    that turns on Explain/Hint/Quiz/Coach), and only ever
                    available once you've scoped a conversation to a
                    specific task — never in the general, curriculum-wide AI
                    tab chat. Even there, if your message reads like a
                    request for a full solution (things like "give me the
                    full answer" or "write all the code"), the assistant
                    proactively declines and offers a hint or an explanation
                    instead, unless this task has explicitly turned Solution
                    on.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section>
                <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-3">
                    What's on by default
                </h2>
                <p class="text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">
                    Unless a task says otherwise: <strong>Explain</strong>,
                    <strong>Hint</strong>, and <strong>Quiz</strong> are on.
                    <strong>Coach</strong> and <strong>Solution</strong> are
                    off. A mode that's off shows as disabled rather than
                    disappearing, so you can always tell it was a deliberate
                    choice by whoever set up the task — not a bug.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section>
                <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-3">
                    Mentor-side assistance isn't one of these
                </h2>
                <p class="text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">
                    Mentors have their own AI assist — a pre-review summary
                    and a mentor-packet assistant — always available to
                    them regardless of a task's policy, since it's a tool
                    for the mentor's own judgment, not something apprentices
                    invoke. It never approves or rejects anything on its
                    own; the mentor remains the authority either way.
                </p>
            </section>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-8 flex gap-4">
            <a href="/docs" class="text-sm text-link hover:underline"
                >← All Docs</a
            >
            <a href="/" class="text-sm text-muted-foreground hover:underline"
                >Back to apprenti.dev</a
            >
        </div>
    </div>
</DocsLayout>
